---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { PracticeScreen } from '../components/Practice/PracticeScreen'
import { SITE } from '../config/site'

const path = '/challenge/'
const title = '60 Second Morse Code Challenge — How Many Can You Decode? | MorseForge'
const description =
  'Sixty seconds, one Morse character at a time. No account, no signup — press start and see how many you can decode. Free.'

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Game',
    name: '60 Second Morse Code Challenge',
    description,
    url: new URL(path, SITE.url).href,
    author: { '@id': `${SITE.url}/#author` },
    gamePlatform: 'Web browser',
    numberOfPlayers: { '@type': 'QuantitativeValue', value: 1 },
  },
]
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} schema={schema}>
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-6 py-14">
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
      60 second Morse challenge
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
      One character at a time, for sixty seconds. Type what you hear. Nothing to sign up for and
      nothing saved anywhere but your own browser.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <PracticeScreen client:load challenge />
    </div>

    <section class="mt-14 border-t border-line pt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-bold tracking-tight">Never done this before?</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Sixty seconds is not much time to learn an alphabet in. Two characters is enough to start
        scoring — E is one dot, T is one dash — and the seven-day plan takes you from there to all
        twenty-six in about two hours.
      </p>
      <a
        href="/learn-morse-code-in-7-days/"
        class="mt-5 inline-block rounded-lg border border-line px-5 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.14em] text-muted transition-colors hover:border-signal hover:text-signal"
        >START WITH DAY 1</a
      >

      <p class="mt-8 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        This one is yours to retake as often as you like. If you want a fixed one instead, the
        <a href="/daily/" class="text-signal underline underline-offset-4">daily challenge</a> is the
        same eight lines for everyone, once a day.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl font-bold tracking-tight">Challenging someone else</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Finish a run and you get a link that carries the whole challenge — length, speed, how many
        characters, and your score to beat:
      </p>
      <pre
        class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line bg-elevated p-4 font-mono text-xs">/challenge/?time=60&amp;wpm=20&amp;count=40&amp;beat=340</pre>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
        There is no server behind it. Everything that defines the run is in the URL, so the link
        works offline, works forever, and cannot be taken down. Values outside a sensible range are
        clamped rather than trusted — a URL is the most editable input a web app has.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
